window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"k":"lesson","t":"01 Control Flow","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/01-control-flow.html","x":"Branching and loops decide which statements run. Branching (if/else/switch) and loops (for/while/do-while) direct which statements run. switch needs break in each case or it falls through to the next case. do-while runs the body at least once (condition checked after). Fall-through gotcha while vs do-while = vs =="},{"k":"question","t":"What does this code print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q1","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When should you use a do-while loop instead of a while loop?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q2","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does this compile, and what is the value of x after the if?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q3","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When is switch fall-through intentional rather than a mistake?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q4","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q5","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a while loop always runs its body at least once.","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q6","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"02 Pointers","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/02-pointers.html","x":"A variable that holds a memory address. A pointer holds an address. * dereferences (read/write the pointed-to value); & takes an address. The pointer variable itself lives on the stack; what it points to may be stack or heap. A pointer can be reseated (change which address it holds) and can be null. The two operators * vs & p vs &p null vs dangling"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between *p and &x?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q7","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"For a heap pointer p, what does printing p give you versus printing &p?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q8","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a null pointer and a dangling pointer?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q9","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Can a pointer be changed to point somewhere else after it is created?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q10","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Where does a pointer variable itself live in memory?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q11","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"After int x = 5; int* p = &x; *p = 9; what is the value of x?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q12","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q13","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/03-stack-and-heap.html","x":"Two regions with very different allocation, speed, and lifetime rules. Stack: memory already committed; allocation = just move the stack pointer. Fast, automatic lifetime tied to scope. High addresses (0x7fff...), grows DOWN. Heap: allocator searches a free list for a block; new/delete. Slower, manual lifetime. Lower addresses, grows UP. push = decrement SP to a lower address; 'top of stack' = lowest address. Returning &local = dangling pointer/UB; mismatched new/delete[] = UB; deep recursion overflows the small (~MB) stack. ASLR randomizes the base address each run. Why the stack is fast Why the heap is flexible but slower stack vs heap grows down vs 'top' &ptr vs ptr"},{"k":"question","t":"Why is stack allocation faster than heap allocation?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q14","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which direction does the stack grow, and where is its 'top'?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q15","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens when the caller dereferences the pointer this function returns?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q16","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens if you free new[] memory with plain delete (instead of delete[])?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q17","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does very deep recursion crash a program?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q18","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do the heap addresses a program prints change on every run?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q19","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In void f() { int a = 1; int* p = new int(2); }, what lives on the heap?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q20","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the stack grows toward lower addresses, so the most recently pushed value sits at the lowest address.","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q21","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"04 References","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/04-references.html","x":"An alias to an existing variable, fixed at declaration. A reference is an alias to an existing variable's address, fixed at declaration — cannot be reseated. Must be initialized when declared; cannot be null (in well-formed code); no reference arithmetic. Pass-by-value = copy (changes don't escape); pass-by-reference (int&) = writes go straight to the caller's variable. Why pass by reference reference vs pointer pass-by-value vs pass-by-reference"},{"k":"question","t":"Name two things a reference cannot do that a pointer can.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q22","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print, and why?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q23","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do we often pass large objects as const T& instead of by value?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q24","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must a reference be initialized at the point where it is declared?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q25","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q26","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: in well-formed code, a reference can be null.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q27","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/05-classes-encapsulation.html","x":"Private data + public methods = one validated gate for every change. Private data + public methods = every change forced through a single gate that validates it → the object can never reach an invalid state (invariant safety). private isn't about security/attackers — it's about controlled change + freedom to change internals later without breaking users. The public method can enforce any rule before writing: no overdraft, clamp ranges, format checks, etc. Invariant private vs security encapsulation vs abstraction"},{"k":"question","t":"If private gives no runtime security, what is the real point of making data private?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q28","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a class invariant?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q29","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Show how a method can protect an invariant that a public field could not.","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q30","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q31","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: making a member private stops other code from reading that object's memory at runtime.","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q32","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"06 Classes vs Structs","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/06-classes-vs-structs.html","x":"Only two real differences; the rest is convention. Only two real language differences: (1) default access — struct public, class private; (2) default inheritance — struct public, class private. Everything else is identical (both can have ctors, methods, virtuals, inheritance, templates). template<class T>/template<typename T> allowed; template<struct T> is not. Convention: struct = passive data bundles; class = encapsulated types with behavior/invariants.  class default vs struct default"},{"k":"question","t":"What are the ONLY two language-level differences between a class and a struct?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q33","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is x accessible from outside here?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q34","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"By convention, when do you use a struct rather than a class?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q35","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are real language differences between class and struct? Select all that apply.","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q36","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"struct B { int x; }; and class D : B {};. Does D d; d.x = 1; compile?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q37","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"07 How to Write a Class","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/07-writing-a-class.html","x":"Split private data vs public interface; mind inline, headers, and const. Decide what's private (data) vs public (interface); outsiders touch data only through methods. Method defined inside the class body = implicitly inline; defined outside needs Class::method. Forgetting Class:: → treated as an unrelated global → linker 'undefined reference'. Header/source split: declarations in .h, definitions in .cpp (ODR — a non-inline body in a header included by multiple TUs = duplicate-symbol error). const-correctness: a method that doesn't modify the object should be const (int GetX() const). ODR — One Definition Rule declaration vs definition inline member vs Class::"},{"k":"question","t":"Why does this compile but fail to link with 'undefined reference to Entity::GetX()'?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q38","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When should a member function be marked const?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q39","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens if you put a non-inline function's full body in a header that multiple .cpp files include?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q40","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a declaration and a definition?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q41","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Entity declares int GetX() const;. Which out-of-class definition is correct?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q42","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a member function defined inside the class body is implicitly inline.","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q43","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"08 Static (outside classes)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/08-static-outside-classes.html","x":"File-scope static = internal linkage; static local = persists across calls. static at file/global scope → internal linkage: symbol visible only in that translation unit (opposite of extern). Two same-named non-static globals defined in two TUs → duplicate-symbol link error; bare extern int x; with no definition → 'undefined reference'. static local variable → initialized once on first entry, retains value between calls, lifetime = whole program, scope = the function. Thread-safe init since C++11. Global static init order across TUs is unspecified ('static initialization order fiasco'); static locals dodge it (init on first use).  static (file) vs extern static local vs normal local"},{"k":"question","t":"What does calling counter() three times in a row print?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q44","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does static do to a variable declared at file/global scope?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q45","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static local variable initialize safely regardless of other globals' init order?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q46","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does extern int x; declare?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q47","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q48","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"a.cpp has static int helper = 5; at file scope. b.cpp says extern int helper; and uses it. What happens?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q49","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/09-static-in-classes.html","x":"One shared copy for all objects; static methods have no `this`. Static member variable = one shared copy for all objects, lives in static storage → does NOT add to object sizeof. Classic use: instance counter. A non-inline static data member must be defined once outside the class (int Entity::count = 0;) or → 'undefined reference'. C++17 inline static avoids the separate definition. Static member function has no this → callable as Class::fn() without an object, and can only access static members.  static member vs instance member static method vs normal method"},{"k":"question","t":"Does a static data member increase each object's sizeof?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q50","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does this fail to link with 'undefined reference to Entity::count'?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q51","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can't a static member function read a normal (non-static) data member?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q52","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On x86-64, what does this print?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q53","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a static member function can read the object's non-static data members.","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q54","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"10 Enums","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/10-enums.html","x":"Named integer constants; prefer `enum class`. Enum = names for integer constants. Values start at 0 and auto-increment unless assigned (enum{A=3,B,C} → B=4, C=5). Under the hood it's just an int; set the underlying type for size: enum Color : uint8_t {...} (1 byte vs default 4). Plain enum: names leak into the enclosing scope (collisions) + implicit int conversion (silent bugs). enum class (C++11): names scoped (Color::Red), no implicit int conversion (needs static_cast). Preferred.  enum vs enum class"},{"k":"question","t":"In enum E { A = 3, B, C };, what are the values of B and C?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q55","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two problems with a plain enum that enum class fixes.","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q56","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you make an enum occupy only 1 byte instead of the default 4?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q57","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you convert an enum class value to an int?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q58","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q59","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"enum class Color { Red, Green }; What does int x = Color::Green; do?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q60","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"11 Constructors","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/11-constructors.html","x":"Special member that sets up a valid object; watch the free default and init lists. Special member, same name as class, no return type, runs automatically on creation; job = valid initial state / acquire resources / enforce invariants. Compiler supplies a free default (no-arg) ctor ONLY if you write zero ctors. Write any ctor → free default is gone unless you add Entity() = default;. Member initializer list (: m_X(0)) inits before the body; required for const members, references, and member objects with no default ctor. Most vexing parse: Entity e; = object, but Entity e(); = a function declaration.  init list vs assignment in body Entity e; vs Entity e();"},{"k":"question","t":"Why does Entity e; stop compiling once you add a constructor that takes arguments?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q61","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name two cases where a member initializer list is required, not merely preferred.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q62","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why prefer an initializer list over assigning members inside the constructor body?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q63","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does Entity e(); actually declare?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q64","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which line creates a default-constructed Entity object named e?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q65","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which members must be initialized in the member initializer list? Select all that apply.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q66","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"12 Destructors & RAII","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/12-destructors.html","x":"Automatic cleanup; virtual for polymorphic bases; Rule of 3/5. ~ClassName(), no params/return, exactly one. Runs at end of scope (stack) or on delete (heap). Members destroyed in reverse order; derived dtor before base. RAII: tie a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime → cleanup is automatic and exception-safe. Virtual destructor required on any polymorphic base: delete a derived object via a base pointer with a non-virtual dtor = UB / leak. Rule of 3/5: custom dtor ⇒ likely need custom copy ctor + copy assignment (+ move ops in C++11), else shallow copy → double-free. Why RAII is the C++ way virtual vs non-virtual destructor shallow vs deep copy"},{"k":"question","t":"Base has a non-virtual destructor — what happens when delete b; runs?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q67","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does RAII make resource cleanup automatic and exception-safe?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q68","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the Rule of Three?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q69","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are members and base/derived subobjects destroyed?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q70","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q71","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: deleting a derived object through a base pointer is safe even when the base destructor is not virtual.","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q72","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"13 Inheritance","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/13-inheritance.html","x":"is-a reuse; base subobject first; watch object slicing. Derived class takes on base members; code reuse + models is-a (Audi is-a Car). Contrast has-a = composition. A derived object contains a base subobject (base part laid out first); construction base→derived, destruction derived→base. Modes: public (true is-a), protected, private (default for class). protected member = accessible to the class's own methods AND derived classes, not outside. Object slicing: assigning derived → base by value copies only the base part → use base pointers/references for polymorphism.  is-a vs has-a protected vs private slicing vs polymorphism"},{"k":"question","t":"What is object slicing?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q73","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Who can access a protected member?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q74","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are the base and derived parts constructed and destroyed?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q75","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an 'is-a' and a 'has-a' relationship?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q76","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q77","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q78","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"14 Virtual Functions","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/14-virtual-functions.html","x":"Dynamic dispatch via vtable/vptr; use override and final. Without virtual, basePtr->fn() uses static dispatch → picks by pointer type (base version). virtual → dynamic dispatch → picks by actual object type at runtime. vtable = per-class array of function pointers (one slot per virtual fn); overridden slots point to derived versions. vptr = hidden per-object pointer to its class's vtable. Only virtual functions are in the vtable; a class with zero virtuals has no vtable/vptr (no size cost). Cost: vptr (~8 bytes/object) + one indirection + usually no inlining. Use override (compiler verifies you match a base virtual) and final (stop further overriding). How a virtual call works static vs dynamic dispatch vtable vs vptr override vs overload"},{"k":"question","t":"What does a->speak() print here, and why?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q79","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does a virtual call select the right function at runtime, using the vtable and vptr?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q80","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the cost of using virtual functions?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q81","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the override specifier do, and why is it worth adding?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q82","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does final do in C++?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q83","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q84","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A class has no virtual functions. How much does a vptr add to each object?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q85","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/15-interfaces-abstract-classes.html","x":"Pure virtuals make a class abstract; all-pure = an interface. A class with ≥1 pure virtual (virtual void f() = 0;) is abstract → cannot be instantiated. All functions pure + no data = an interface (convention). You CAN use base pointers/refs to an abstract class — that's the point: Base* p = new Derived(); works if Derived overrides every pure virtual. An abstract base can still provide implemented (normal virtual) methods → shared behavior; pure virtuals force each derived to supply its own. An interface needs a virtual destructor.  abstract class vs interface pure virtual vs virtual"},{"k":"question","t":"What makes a class abstract in C++?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q86","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If you can't instantiate an abstract class, what CAN you do with it?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q87","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface in C++?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q88","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must an interface / polymorphic base declare a virtual destructor?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q89","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Can an abstract class also contain normal (non-pure) methods with bodies?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q90","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; };, which line does not compile?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q91","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a derived class that overrides only some of its base's pure virtual functions is still abstract.","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q92","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/16-const-constexpr-volatile.html","x":"Read-only vs compile-time-known vs don't-optimize. const = won't change after initialization (but CAN be set at runtime). constexpr = value/function evaluable at compile time (stronger). Every constexpr is const, not vice versa. constexpr function: runs at compile time with compile-time args, else at runtime. volatile = don't optimize accesses; re-read from memory every time (value may change externally). Not for thread sync — use std::atomic. const vs constexpr const vs constexpr constexpr vs volatile volatile vs atomic"},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these compile, and which is an error?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q93","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the core difference between const and constexpr?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q94","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is volatile a correct tool for synchronizing a variable between threads?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q95","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When does a constexpr function run at runtime instead of compile time?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q96","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"n is read at runtime (int n = getInput();). Which of these lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q97","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: marking a shared counter volatile makes ++counter safe to call from two threads.","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q98","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/17-compile-time-constants-and-vla.html","x":"Why int arr[n] with a runtime n is not standard C++. constexpr int s = 5*4; int a[s]; → legal, portable (compile-time constant). const int s = 5*4; int a[s]; → also legal in C++ (const + constant initializer). int s = 5*4; int a[s]; → NOT standard C++ — a VLA (Variable Length Array). VLAs are valid in C (C99); g++/clang allow them as a non-standard extension; MSVC rejects them. -pedantic surfaces the warning in EVERY C++ standard (98→20); the flag, not the version. Prove it with the compiler const int size vs plain int size compiler allows vs standard allows"},{"k":"question","t":"Two of these are standard C++ and one is not — which one, and what is it called?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q99","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Your int a[n] (runtime n) compiled fine on g++ — how do you check whether it's actually standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q100","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does the VLA warning depend on which C++ version you pick (-std=c++11 vs c++20)?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q101","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which array sizes ARE valid standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q102","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What runtime clue reveals that an array is actually a VLA?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q103","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"int a[n]; with a runtime n compiles silently on g++. Which flag makes g++ warn that it is not standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q104","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: switching from -std=c++11 to -std=c++20 makes g++ reject int a[n]; with a runtime n.","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q105","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/18-arrays-and-contiguity.html","x":"Contiguous in virtual space; scattered in physical RAM. int is 4 bytes → an array of 100,000 ints ≈ 400 KB. An array MUST be contiguous: arr[i] is computed as base + i*sizeof(int) — pointer arithmetic depends on it. Contiguity is required in virtual address space, not physical RAM. The array's pages can sit in scattered frames; the page table makes them appear contiguous. Big arrays belong on the heap (new/vector); a large local array can overflow the ~1–8 MB stack. With demand paging, virtual space is reserved immediately but physical frames arrive lazily on first touch. Why scattered-physical is fine virtual-contiguous vs physical-scattered stack array vs heap array"},{"k":"question","t":"An array must be laid out contiguously — is that guaranteed in virtual memory or in physical RAM?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q106","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does the compiler compute the address of arr[i]?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q107","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"An int arr[100000] is about 400 KB — should it live on the stack or the heap, and why?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q108","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With demand paging, is the physical RAM for a big new array allocated immediately?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q109","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q110","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"An array must be contiguous. Where is that actually guaranteed?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q111","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"19 Endianness","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/19-endianness.html","x":"Byte order of multi-byte values; only visible in raw bytes. Endianness = the order bytes of a multi-byte value are stored. Little-endian stores least-significant byte first (x86/x64, most ARM); big-endian stores most-significant first. The value (how we write a number, MSB first) looks like big-endian; little-endian storage is reversed — flip to read it. Applies to EVERY multi-byte value (int, pointer). A pointer is just an 8-byte integer → stored little-endian too. Only visible in raw bytes (hex dump, char*, cross-machine data). cout/printf show the actual number, never reversed. Single bytes (char) are unaffected; network byte order is big-endian (htonl/ntohl). Reading a dump little vs big endian stored bytes vs the value cout pointer vs raw bytes"},{"k":"question","t":"On a little-endian machine, how is the int 500 (0x000001F4) laid out byte by byte in memory?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q112","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If you cout << somePointer;, do you see the reversed little-endian bytes or the real address?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q113","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is a pointer affected by endianness?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q114","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you detect at runtime whether a machine is little- or big-endian?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q115","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which values are immune to endianness?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q116","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On a little-endian machine (x86-64), what does this print?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q117","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on a little-endian machine, std::cout << x; prints the value of x with its bytes reversed.","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q118","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/20-undefined-behavior-and-bounds.html","x":"[] doesn't check; .at() throws; a passing run is not proof. arr[i] and array::operator[] do NO bounds checking → out-of-range access is undefined behavior (UB). UB can look like it works (may print a value, may crash, may corrupt) — 'it printed 4' is NOT proof of correctness. array::at(i) / vector::at(i) are bounds-checked → throw std::out_of_range. Catch bugs with sanitizers: -fsanitize=address,undefined. C++ philosophy: 'don't pay for what you don't use' — speed by default, safety opt-in via .at(). Why [] is unchecked [] vs .at() UB vs a controlled crash 'works' vs correct"},{"k":"question","t":"This program prints 4 and exits cleanly. Is it correct?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q119","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which access is bounds-checked, and what does it do on a bad index?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q120","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does C++ leave operator[] unchecked by default?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q121","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name three different things undefined behaviour is allowed to do.","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q122","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which compiler flag would have caught the out-of-bounds write at runtime during testing?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q123","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"std::array<int, 5> a{}; a[10] = 4; What does the C++ standard say happens?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q124","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q125","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"21 std::array vs std::vector","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/21-array-vs-vector.html","x":"Fixed size vs growable; pick by whether the count is known. std::array<T,N> = fixed size baked in at compile time; cannot grow. Lives on the stack (no heap). std::vector<T> = dynamic; grows at runtime with push_back/resize. Backed by the heap. Both offer [] (unchecked) and .at() (checked), and .size() to loop safely. Fixed, known count → array. Varies/grows → vector. Choosing array vs vector size vs capacity"},{"k":"question","t":"When should you choose std::array rather than std::vector?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q126","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does a std::vector grow when you keep adding elements?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q127","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a vector's size() and its capacity()?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q128","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Do both array and vector offer a bounds-checked access?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q129","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can grow at runtime with push_back?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q130","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q131","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/22-exceptions-try-catch.html","x":"Throw signals an error; catch handles it; uncaught = abort. throw raises an exception; a matching catch handles it. .at() throws std::out_of_range on a bad index. An uncaught exception propagates to std::terminate → 'Aborted (core dumped)'. That crash means nobody handled it. Wrap risky code in try { ... } catch (const std::exception& e) { ... }; e.what() gives the message. Catch by const reference (avoids slicing and copies). Handle vs abort uncaught throw vs bug in .at() catch by value vs by reference"},{"k":"question","t":"Calling a.at(10) on a size-5 array printed 'Aborted (core dumped)'. Whose fault is the crash?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q132","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Rewrite the call so the program reports the error and keeps running.","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q133","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Should you catch an exception by value or by const reference, and why?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q134","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does e.what() return?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q135","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q136","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which is the recommended way to catch a standard exception?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q137","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/23-sanitizers-and-debugging.html","x":"A runtime detector that plants tripwires around your memory. Compile with -fsanitize=address -g; ASan is a runtime detector — it reports only when a bad access actually executes (a clean program prints nothing). It surrounds variables with poisoned redzones; touching one = flagged (stack-buffer-overflow, heap-use-after-free, etc.). By default it aborts on the first error; ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=0 continues. The report shows the exact line, the variable, and its byte offsets (e.g. [32,52) 'a' = 20 bytes = 5 ints). Companion tools: -fsanitize=undefined (UBSan), gdb, valgrind. Reading the report compile-time vs runtime detection ASan redzone vs stack canary"},{"k":"question","t":"You built with -fsanitize=address and compiling produced no error — why hasn't AddressSanitizer reported anything yet?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q138","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does AddressSanitizer detect an out-of-bounds access?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q139","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does AddressSanitizer report every error in a run, or stop at the first one?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q140","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name two other tools or flags for finding C++ memory / undefined-behaviour bugs.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q141","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which flags build a program with AddressSanitizer and the line numbers it needs for a readable report?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q142","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a program built with -fsanitize=address compiles without errors, it has no memory bugs.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q143","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/24-paging-and-virtual-memory.html","x":"Chop the process into pages so it needn't be contiguous. A process's virtual space is cut into equal pages; physical RAM into equal frames of the same size. Each page is stored in one frame. One frame holds exactly one page (one big 'ball' fills the box). Different regions (code/heap/stack) land in different frames. Paging removes the need for one big contiguous block → solves external fragmentation. The program sees a clean contiguous virtual layout; the pages sit in scattered physical frames. The page table connects them. Bonuses: virtual memory (run bigger than RAM), isolation/protection, sharing (one library frame for many processes). Page vs frame page vs frame virtual vs physical address contiguous-virtual vs scattered-physical"},{"k":"question","t":"Why is a process divided into pages in the first place?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q144","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a page and a frame?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q145","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does virtual address space itself store any data or hold any RAM?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q146","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If a program's pages are scattered all over physical RAM, how does it still behave as if memory were one contiguous block?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q147","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Beyond avoiding fragmentation, what benefits does paging unlock?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q148","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: one physical frame can hold pieces of several pages at the same time.","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q149","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which problem does paging solve?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q150","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/25-mmu-tlb-multilevel-paging.html","x":"Hardware translation, a cache for it, and a sparse page-table tree. The MMU is hardware in the CPU that translates virtual→physical on every access, and checks permission bits (raising page/protection faults). A virtual address = page number + offset: page number → page table → frame number; frame + offset → physical address. The TLB is the MMU's small cache of recent page→frame translations, so most accesses skip the table walk. A single flat page table would be huge (x86-64: ~512 GB per process) → multi-level paging: a sparse tree of small tables, only allocating branches that are used. x86-64 uses 4 levels (9+9+9+9 bits + 12-bit offset); each table has 512 entries. Why a tree instead of one table MMU vs TLB page number vs offset flat vs multi-level page table"},{"k":"question","t":"What does the MMU do on every memory access?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q151","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A virtual address splits into two parts for translation — what are they, and what does each select?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q152","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why don't OSes use a single flat page table (on x86-64)?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q153","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How many levels does x86-64 paging use, and how is the address split?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q154","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A multi-level walk costs several memory accesses per translation — what hides that cost?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q155","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With 4 KB (4096-byte) pages, virtual address 12290 is in which page, and at what offset?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q156","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With 4 KB pages, how many bits of a virtual address are the offset inside the page?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q157","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/26-memory-allocation-and-fragmentation.html","x":"Contiguous vs paging vs segmentation; internal vs external waste. Allocation schemes: contiguous (one block per process), and non-contiguous — paging (fixed pages) and segmentation (variable logical segments). External fragmentation = wasted gaps BETWEEN allocations (contiguous & segmentation suffer it). Paging cures it. Internal fragmentation = wasted space INSIDE the last page of a region (paging causes a little; ≤ page size − 1). Internal frag hits only the last page because every earlier page is completely full. External fragmentation is a problem, not a method — you don't choose it; contiguous allocation suffers it, paging avoids it. Segmentation in one line internal vs external fragmentation paging vs segmentation problem vs method"},{"k":"question","t":"Where is the wasted space in internal fragmentation versus external fragmentation?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q158","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does paging cause internal fragmentation but eliminate external fragmentation?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q159","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does internal fragmentation only affect the last page of a region?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q160","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is external fragmentation a memory-management method you can choose, like paging?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q161","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does each entry in a segment table store?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q162","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A process needs 10 KB and pages are 4 KB. How many frames does it use, and how much internal fragmentation is there?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q163","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: paging suffers from external fragmentation.","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q164","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/27-demand-paging-and-swapping.html","x":"Load pages lazily; overflow cold pages to disk. Demand paging: don't load the whole program up front — load each page only on first use. Touching a not-present page raises a page fault: the OS grabs a free frame, loads the page from disk, updates the table, restarts the instruction — invisibly. A page fault is expensive (disk ~1000× slower than RAM) but paid once per page, then amortized over many fast reuses (locality of reference). Swapping: when RAM is full, evict a cold page to disk (swap) to free a frame — this lets a program use more memory than physical RAM (virtual memory). Working set > RAM → constant faults = thrashing (severe slowdown). Minor vs major fault demand paging vs swapping minor vs major page fault page fault vs segfault"},{"k":"question","t":"Walk through exactly what happens on a page fault.","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q165","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A page fault can be ~1000× slower than a RAM access — why is demand paging still a net win?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q166","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is thrashing, and what causes it?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q167","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a minor and a major page fault?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q168","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How is a page fault different from a segmentation fault?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q169","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is swapping, and which capability does it provide?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q170","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true of a page fault? Select all that apply.","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q171","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"The running programs need more memory than RAM, so pages are constantly swapped in and out and the system crawls. What is this called?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q172","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","c":"Linux","u":"html/28-linux-filesystem-navigation.html","x":"One tree from /, absolute vs relative paths. Single tree rooted at /; no drive letters. Absolute path starts at /; relative path starts from the current directory. . = here, .. = parent. Relative paths in code (open(\"config.txt\")) resolve against the process's working directory — a common 'file not found' bug. Commands: pwd (where am I), ls -la (list, long, all), cd .. (up one).  absolute vs relative path . vs .."},{"k":"question","t":"Your program does open(\"config.txt\"); it works from one directory but fails with 'file not found' from another. Why?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q173","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an absolute and a relative path?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q174","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What do . and .. refer to in a path?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q175","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does ls -la show that a plain ls does not?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q176","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Both directories exist. What does this print?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q177","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these paths is absolute?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q178","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"29 Permissions (rwx)","c":"Linux","u":"html/29-linux-permissions.html","x":"Read the 10-char mode; r=4 w=2 x=1. ls -l line: 10 chars = 1 file-type (- file, d dir, l symlink) + three rwx triplets for owner / group / others. r=4, w=2, x=1; add per triplet → numeric modes like 755 (rwxr-xr-x), 644 (rw-r--r--). A binary needs the x bit to run with ./; a source .cpp is usually 644 (no x), a compiled binary 755.  owner vs group vs others r/w/x on a file vs a directory"},{"k":"question","t":"What numeric permission mode does -rwxr-xr-x correspond to?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q179","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You compiled a program but ./prog says 'Permission denied' — what's the most likely cause?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q180","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When you access a file, how does the kernel decide which of the three rwx triplets to apply?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q181","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On a directory, what do r, w, and x each mean (they differ from a file)?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q182","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is -rw-r--r-- in numeric form?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q183","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"ls -l shows drwxr-x---. What is it, and can others list its contents?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q184","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"30 chmod","c":"Linux","u":"html/30-chmod.html","x":"Change permission bits, numeric or symbolic. Numeric mode sets all three triplets at once: chmod 755 f (rwx/r-x/r-x), chmod 644 f (rw-/r--/r--). Symbolic mode nudges bits: chmod +x f (x for all), chmod u+x f (owner only); u=owner, g=group, o=others, a=all. Missing x = 'Permission denied' when running ./script.sh or a fresh binary — the #1 cause.  numeric vs symbolic chmod chmod vs chown"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between chmod 755 f and chmod +x f?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q185","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In symbolic chmod, how do you give execute permission to ONLY the owner?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q186","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which permission bit is the usual culprit behind 'Permission denied' when running ./script.sh?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q187","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A file is -rw-r--r-- (644). What does ls -l show after chmod u+x f?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q188","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which command sets a file to exactly rwxr-xr-x, whatever its bits were before?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q189","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"31 chown & Ownership","c":"Linux","u":"html/31-chown-ownership.html","x":"Change owner/group; only root can give a file away. chown user file, chown user:group file, chown :group file (group only); -R = recursive. chgrp group file changes only the group. Only root (sudo) can change a file's owner — a regular user can't give their file away or take another's. The owner CAN change the group to one they belong to. Ownership + rwx triplets together gate access: a process's UID/GID is checked against owner/group/other bits on every open/read/write. id = your UID/GID/groups; whoami = your username. Systems relevance: deploying a service = chown binary/data to a service user; setuid / privilege-dropping daemons rely on ownership.  chown vs chgrp id vs whoami"},{"k":"question","t":"Why can't a normal (non-root) user give their own file to another user with chown?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q190","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give the chown syntax for: owner+group, owner only, group only, and recursive.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q191","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do ownership and the rwx bits work together to decide access?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q192","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does id show that whoami does not?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q193","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a regular (non-root) user can give one of their own files to another user with chown.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q194","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which command changes only the group of data.txt to devs?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q195","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"32 Member Initializer List","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/32-member-initializer-list.html","x":"Initialize class members directly, once — and the only way to reach a base ctor. Written after the constructor's : and before {}; initializes members directly, once (vs default-then-assign in the body). Mandatory for const members, reference members, and members whose type has no default constructor. The only way to pass arguments to a base class constructor — even inside a default constructor. Members initialize in declaration order, NOT the order written in the list. Delegating constructor (C++11): one ctor calls another; it must be the only item in the list. Default member initializer (C++11, int a = 0;) is a fallback; the list takes precedence over it (default simply not used, not overridden). Body can assign later, but never to const/reference members. One example that shows every point initialize (list) vs assign (body) declaration order vs list order default member initializer vs MIL"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between initialization (in the list) and assignment (in the body)?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q196","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which members MUST be initialized in the list (won't compile otherwise)?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q197","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you pass an argument to a base class constructor?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q198","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are members initialized?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q199","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a delegating constructor, and its one rule?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q200","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If C++11 lets you write int a = 0; at the declaration, why is the init list still needed?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q201","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q202","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a member has a default initializer (int a = 0;) and is also in the member initializer list, the list's value is used.","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q203","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"33 Ternary Operator","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/33-ternary-operator.html","x":"The conditional operator — pick a value inline with cond ? a : b. Real name = conditional operator; the only operator with three operands. Syntax: cond ? a : b. It's an expression (yields a value), not a statement — so it works inline: init a variable, a const, a return, a function argument. Only the chosen branch is evaluated (safe for guarded access like p ? *p : 0). Both branches must share a common type; if they differ but convert (int/double) the result is the common type — no common type = error. Low precedence: wrap it in parentheses with << -> cout << (c ? \"a\" : \"b\");. Nesting/chaining is legal but hurts readability; prefer if/else or switch beyond one condition. One example that shows the key points ternary vs if/else same type vs common type ?: vs << precedence"},{"k":"question","t":"Is the ternary a statement or an expression, and why does that matter?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q204","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can a ternary initialize a const when if/else can't?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q205","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In cond ? 1 : 2.5, what is the result type and why?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q206","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When do the two branches make a ternary fail to compile?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q207","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does cout << c ? \"a\" : \"b\"; need parentheses?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q208","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is the branch that isn't chosen evaluated?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q209","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q210","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"auto r = true ? 1 : 2.5; What is the type of r?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q211","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/34-creating-objects.html","x":"An object lives on the stack or the heap — and that choice sets its lifetime. An object lives on the stack or the heap; that choice decides its lifetime. Stack: Entity e; — automatic lifetime (destroyed at end of scope, dtor runs), fast, small; access with .. Heap: Entity* e = new Entity; — new returns a pointer; the object lives until you delete it (else memory leak); access with ->. Use the heap when the object must outlive its scope, is too big for the stack, is sized at runtime, or needs polymorphism (Base* b = new Derived;). Traps: dangling pointer (points to a destroyed/ freed object), leak (never deleted), double-delete. Set the pointer to nullptr after delete (delete nullptr; is safe). Entity e(); is a function declaration (most vexing parse) — use Entity e; or Entity e{};. One example: stack, heap, and a dangling pointer stack object vs heap object . vs -> dangling vs leak"},{"k":"question","t":"What are the two places an object can live, and how does each affect its lifetime?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q212","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do you access a heap object with -> but a stack object with .?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q213","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a memory leak and what causes it?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q214","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a dangling pointer? Give two ways to create one.","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q215","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why set a pointer to nullptr after delete?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q216","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does Entity e(); actually declare?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q217","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Entity* e = new Entity; How do you call its print() method?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q218","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these leak memory or use a dangling pointer? Select all that apply.","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q219","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"35 The new Keyword","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/35-new-keyword.html","x":"new = allocate on the heap + run the constructor; delete = destructor + free. new is an operator that does two things: allocates heap memory (via operator new, usually malloc) and calls the constructor. Returns a typed pointer. delete = destructor + free. Sizes: new int (4B, garbage), new int() (4B, zero-init), new int[n] (n can be a runtime value — dynamic array). Pairing (mismatch = UB): new↔delete, new[]↔delete[], malloc↔free. Never cross them. new vs malloc: new calls ctor/dtor, returns a typed pointer (no cast), and throws std::bad_alloc on failure; malloc = raw bytes, void*, returns nullptr, no ctor. Heap layout: a fixed-size header sits before p (stores the block size); free/delete do p - headerSize (offset is a compile-time constant) to find it. new[] also stores a count cookie so delete[] runs every destructor. (Deep dive: the Heap layout page linked at the bottom of this lesson.) Modern C++: prefer smart pointers (make_unique/make_shared) over raw new/delete. Syntax and matching delete new vs malloc delete vs free delete vs delete[] header offset vs block size"},{"k":"question","t":"What two things does new do?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q220","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does new differ from malloc? (three ways)","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q221","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must new[] be paired with delete[] and not plain delete?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q222","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between delete and free?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q223","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"free/delete are given only p — how do they know where the header is and how big the block is?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q224","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between new int; and new int();?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q225","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q226","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true of new compared with malloc? Select all that apply.","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q227","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/36-implicit-conversion-explicit.html","x":"A single-arg constructor lets the compiler auto-convert; explicit turns that off. A single-argument constructor is a converting constructor: the compiler can use it to auto-convert that arg type into your class (Entity e = 22;). Implicit conversion also fires in function arguments and return values, not just assignments. Rule: at most one user-defined conversion in a chain — so Entity e = 22; works (one), but Entity e = \"Anup\"; fails (char* -> string -> Entity = two). explicit on a constructor disables implicit conversion through it; you must construct directly (Entity e(22);) or cast ((Entity)22). Why: implicit conversions can silently hide bugs. Guideline: mark single-arg constructors explicit unless you deliberately want the conversion. Direct init Entity e(22); always works; copy init Entity e = 22; is what explicit blocks. explicit also applies to conversion operators (C++11). Where it fires, and where explicit stops it implicit vs explicit conversion direct init vs copy init one vs two user conversions"},{"k":"question","t":"What makes a constructor a 'converting constructor'?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q228","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Exactly why does Entity e = \"Anup\"; fail?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q229","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the explicit keyword do?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q230","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With an explicit constructor, which works: Entity e = 22; or Entity e(22);? Why?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q231","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is marking single-argument constructors explicit often a good idea?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q232","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given struct Entity { explicit Entity(int a); }; and void f(Entity);, which lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q233","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q234","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/37-operator-overloading.html","x":"Define what operators mean for your own type — chosen by operand types at compile time. An operator is a function with a special name (operator+, operator==, operator<<); overloading = defining what it does for your type. It's compile-time polymorphism (the compiler picks the function from the operand types). Two forms: member (left operand is this) and free function (both operands are params). Use a free function when the left operand isn't your class (cout << e, 2 * v). operator<< must be a free function (left operand is the stream, which you don't own) and returns std::ostream& so calls chain (cout << a << b). Params as const T& (reference = no copy, const = don't modify the argument); trailing const on a member = doesn't modify this. Return by value for arithmetic (+ makes a NEW object); return *this by reference for assignment/compound (=, +=) so a = b = c chains. Limits: can't invent operators or change precedence/arity; at least one operand must be user-defined (int+int can't be redefined); can't overload ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof. Examples: + (by value), = (by reference), << (free) member vs free operator + returns by value vs = returns by reference overloadable vs not"},{"k":"question","t":"What is operator overloading, and why is it called compile-time polymorphism?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q235","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When must you use a free (non-member) operator instead of a member?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q236","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must operator<< be a free function and return std::ostream&?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q237","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does operator+ return by value but operator= returns *this by reference?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q238","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two limits on operator overloading.","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q239","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q240","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these operators can not be overloaded?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q241","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"38 The this Keyword","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/38-this-keyword.html","x":"A pointer to the current object, available inside non-static member functions. this is a pointer available in every non-static member function, holding the address of the object the function was called on. Type: non-const method -> Entity* const (can change the object, can't reseat the pointer); const method -> const Entity* const (can't change the object either). this = the pointer (address); *this = the object. Use this->m or (*this).m; return *this by reference (Entity&) for chaining — returning it by value copies. Main uses: disambiguate a member from a same-named parameter (this->a = a;), return *this for chaining, and self-comparison (this == &other). this-> is optional for member access — needed only to disambiguate a same-named parameter (or dependent names in templates). Static member functions have no this (not tied to an object). The compiler passes this as a hidden first argument to each non-static call. The three main uses of this this vs *this non-const vs const member this this-> optional vs required"},{"k":"question","t":"What is this, and where is it available?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q242","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the type of this in a non-const vs a const member function?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q243","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between this and *this?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q244","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When do you actually need to write this->?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q245","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static member function have no this?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q246","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q247","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"This version returns Counter by value instead of by reference. What does it print now?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q248","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"39 Object Lifetime","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/39-object-lifetime.html","x":"Automatic, dynamic, or static — how long an object lives, and RAII. Three storage durations: automatic (stack — lives to the end of its {} scope, dtor runs automatically), dynamic (heap — from new until delete, else leak), static/global (whole program). 'Lifetime = scope' applies only to automatic objects; within a scope they're destroyed in reverse order of creation. Returning &x / a reference to a local = dangling (its stack frame is gone). Fixes: return by value, heap + transfer ownership, or a caller-supplied buffer. RAII: acquire a resource in the constructor, release it in the destructor -> cleanup is automatic and exception-safe (destructors run during stack unwinding). Basis of smart pointers, lock_guard, fstream. C++ gives deterministic cleanup — objects die at predictable scope-exit points (unlike garbage-collected languages). A temporary lives to the end of the full expression (;); a temporary bound to a const reference has its lifetime extended to the reference. Dangling vs valid, RAII, and a static local automatic vs dynamic vs static lifetime dangling vs valid return RAII vs manual cleanup"},{"k":"question","t":"Name the three storage durations and what determines each object's lifetime.","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q249","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is returning the address of a local variable a bug?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q250","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two correct ways to return data that outlives a function.","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q251","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is RAII, and why is it exception-safe?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q252","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static local variable keep its value between calls?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q253","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (Each destructor prints its id.)","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q254","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which function returns a dangling reference?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q255","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"40 Smart Pointers","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/40-smart-pointers.html","x":"RAII wrappers that own a heap object and delete it automatically. Smart pointers (in <memory>) are RAII wrappers around a raw pointer: they own the heap object and auto-delete it when they go out of scope — no manual delete, leaks, or dangling. unique_ptr = sole owner; can't be copied, only moved (std::move); near-zero overhead. Your default. Create with make_unique. shared_ptr = shared ownership via a reference count; the object is deleted when the count hits 0 (use_count()). Create with make_shared (one allocation). Has a control-block overhead. weak_ptr = non-owning observer of a shared_ptr; does NOT change the count. Used to break circular references and to check/access via .lock() (or .expired()). (Deep dive: the shared_ptr circular reference page linked at the bottom of this lesson.) Prefer make_unique/make_shared over raw new (exception-safe, no raw new). Smart pointers overload * and ->, so you use them like raw pointers. Members: .get() (raw pointer — don't delete), .reset() (delete now), unique_ptr::release() (give up ownership WITHOUT deleting — you must delete). Use raw pointers/references only for non-owning borrowing. Creating, moving, sharing, observing unique_ptr vs shared_ptr weak_ptr vs shared_ptr release() vs reset() vs move owning vs non-owning"},{"k":"question","t":"What problem do smart pointers solve, and which mechanism makes them work?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q256","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between unique_ptr and shared_ptr, and how does shared_ptr know when to delete?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q257","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is weak_ptr for, and why doesn't it affect the reference count?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q258","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why prefer make_unique/make_shared over raw new?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q259","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What's the difference between release(), reset(), and std::move on a unique_ptr?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q260","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When is a raw pointer the right choice over a smart pointer?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q261","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q262","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"auto p = std::make_unique<int>(1); auto q = p; What happens?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q263","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/41-copy-constructors.html","x":"Deep-copy a class that owns a resource, or two objects share it and double-free. Copy constructor T(const T& other) runs when a new object is built from an existing one: T b = a;, T b(a);, pass-by-value, return-by-value. The compiler's default copy ctor does a shallow (member-wise) copy — for a pointer member it copies the address, so both objects end up sharing one buffer. Shared buffer -> both destructors delete it -> double-free (and editing one secretly edits the other). A deep copy (allocate your own buffer + copy the contents) fixes it. Rule of Three: if you need a custom destructor, you also need a custom copy constructor and copy assignment (C++11 adds move ctor + move assignment = Rule of Five). Copy constructor builds a new object from an existing one; copy assignment (operator=) is for two objects that already exist (free the old buffer, deep-copy, self-assign guard, return *this). Pass by const& to avoid triggering a copy; = default (ask for the compiler's version) / = delete (forbid copying, like unique_ptr). Deep-copy String (the corrected version) shallow vs deep copy copy constructor vs copy assignment Rule of Three vs Rule of Five"},{"k":"question","t":"Name two situations where the copy constructor is called.","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q264","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the default copy constructor do, and why is that dangerous for a class with a pointer member?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q265","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a deep copy and how does it fix the double-free?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q266","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the Rule of Three (and Five)?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q267","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between the copy constructor and copy assignment?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q268","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (D = default constructor, C = copy constructor, A = copy assignment.)","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q269","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the compiler-generated copy constructor copies the data that a pointer member points to.","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q270","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"42 The Arrow Operator","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/42-arrow-operator.html","x":"ptr->member is shorthand for (*ptr).member — and you can overload it. ptr->member is shorthand for (*ptr).member — dereference the pointer, then apply the dot. . is used on an object (or reference); -> on a pointer (raw or smart). For a raw pointer, -> is built-in. For your own class you can overload operator-> — that's how smart pointers and iterators work. An overloaded operator-> returns a pointer; the compiler then applies the real -> to it (and keeps chaining until it reaches a raw pointer). That's why unique_ptr/shared_ptr let you write p->method() even though p is an object, not a raw pointer. (Curiosity: -> through a null-cast pointer is the trick behind offsetof for a member's byte offset — don't use it in real code.) Raw pointer, and a custom operator-> . vs -> built-in -> vs overloaded ->"},{"k":"question","t":"What is e->a equivalent to, written the long way?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q271","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"For a raw pointer, is -> built-in or overloaded — and when does overloading come in?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q272","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does an overloaded operator-> return, and what does the compiler do with it?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q273","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can you use -> on a smart pointer even though it's an object, not a raw pointer?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q274","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q275","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: an overloaded operator-> must return a pointer, or an object that itself has operator->.","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q276","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/43-std-vector.html","x":"A contiguous, growable heap array — with amortized O(1) push_back. std::vector = the STL's dynamic array: contiguous, growable, elements on the heap (<vector>); [] is O(1) and cache-friendly. size() = elements stored; capacity() = total slots allocated before a reallocation (spare room = capacity - size). On growth it allocates a new, bigger block (~1.5-2x), moves the elements over, and frees the old — never extends in place. So push_back is amortized O(1), and a reallocation invalidates old iterators/pointers/references. [] is unchecked (UB if out of range); .at() is bounds-checked (throws std::out_of_range). push_back(x) copies/moves an already-built object in; emplace_back(args...) constructs the element in place from the args (no temporary). Cut the two costs: reserve(n) (pre-allocate to the expected size -> no repeated reallocations) and emplace_back (no temporary copy/destroy). Pass by const& to avoid copying the whole vector. Common usage Why amortized O(1) (doubling vs +1 growth) size() vs capacity() push_back vs emplace_back [] vs .at() amortized O(1) vs O(1)"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between size() and capacity()?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q277","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens when push_back exceeds capacity, and why is it still amortized O(1)?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q278","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does reserve() optimize?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q279","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between push_back and emplace_back?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q280","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between v[i] and v.at(i)?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q281","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why are existing iterators/pointers/references invalidated after a vector grows?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q282","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q283","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You saved int* p = &v[0];, then a push_back made the vector reallocate. What is p now?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q284","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"44 Local Static Variables","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/44-local-static.html","x":"static on a local variable: scope stays in the function, lifetime becomes the whole program. static on a local variable changes only its lifetime. Scope stays inside the function, so it acts like a private global. It lives in the static area (.data / .bss), not on the stack, so it keeps its value between calls. A constant initializer (static int n = 0;) is set before main, with no run-time work and no flag. A run-time initializer (a constructor, new) runs once, the first time execution reaches the line, behind a hidden flag (thread-safe since C++11). It is destroyed at program exit, in reverse order of construction. Only the first build is thread-safe. Changing the value from two threads is still a data race. One example for all the points scope vs lifetime static local vs plain local static int n = 0; vs static Logger L; static Logger L; vs static Logger* P = new Logger();"},{"k":"question","t":"What changes when you put static on a local variable, and what stays the same?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q285","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does static Logger L; need a hidden flag but static int n = 0; does not?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q286","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this code print, and why?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q287","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is returning arr safe with static int arr[3] but not with int arr[3]?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q288","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function with static Logger L; is never called. Is the constructor ever run? Is the destructor?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q289","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Two threads call a function containing static Logger L; at the same time. What is safe and what is not?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q290","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between static Logger L; and static Logger* P = new Logger(); at program exit?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q291","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q292","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: other functions in the same file can use a static local variable by name.","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q293","x":""},{"k":"deep dive","t":"Heap layout — new / delete / malloc / free","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/heap-layout-new-malloc.html","x":"What sits in memory around a heap block and how delete finds its size."},{"k":"deep dive","t":"shared_ptr circular reference — why it leaks","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/shared-ptr-cycle.html","x":"Why two shared_ptrs pointing at each other never free, and how weak_ptr breaks the cycle."},{"k":"deep dive","t":"From written code to execution","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/code-to-execution-road.html","x":"End-to-end road from source code through compile, link, load and paging to running instructions."}];